			<div class="club-section">
				<h2>Degenerated case</h2>

				A naive BST implementation could lead to create a case where the
				tree is skewed, i.e., it looks like a linked list. This is
				called a degenerated case. In this case, the time complexity of
				the operations becomes O(n). Here is an animation of how the
				degenerated case looks like:
				<div class="club-image">
					<video autoplay loop muted playsinline controls>
						<source
							src={`${base}/media/bst_degenerated.mp4`}
							type="video/mp4"
						/>
						Your browser does not support the video tag.
					</video>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="club-section">
				<h2>Balanced BST</h2>
				To avoid the degenerated case, we can use a balanced BST. A balanced
				BST is a BST that is balanced, i.e., it is not skewed. In a balanced
				BST, the height of the tree is O(log n). There are several types of
				balanced BSTs, such as AVL trees, Red-Black trees, etc. Fortunately,
				we don't need to implement a balanced BST ourselves (for now...).
				We can use the built-in data structures in C++ that are based on balanced
				BSTs, such as std::set, std::map, std::multiset, and std::multimap.
				these structures are implemented over a red-black tree, which is a
				type of balanced BST.

				This implementation also guarantees that the elements will
				always be sorted.
			</div>

			<div class="club-section">
				<h2>std::set</h2>
				<p>
					std::set is an associative container that contains a sorted
					set of unique objects of type Key. One of the properties we
					like the most about the set is that it not contains
					duplicates.
				</p>
				<h2>std::set operations</h2>
				<p>
					In C++, <code>std::set</code> is a container that stores unique
					elements following a specific order. It's implemented as a balanced
					BST (typically Red-Black Tree), making it very useful for competitive
					programming.
				</p>

				<table class="operations-table">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Operation</th>
							<th>Complexity</th>
							<th>What it does</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>insert(x)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td
								>Inserts element x into the set (no duplicates)</td
							>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>erase(x)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Removes element x from the set</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>erase(it)</code></td>
							<td>O(1) amortized</td>
							<td>Removes element at iterator position</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>find(x)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Returns iterator to x, or end() if not found</td
							>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>count(x)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Returns 1 if x exists, 0 otherwise</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>lower_bound(x)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Returns iterator to first element ≥ x</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>upper_bound(x)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Returns iterator to first element > x</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>begin()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns iterator to smallest element</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>end()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns iterator past the largest element</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>rbegin()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns reverse iterator to largest element</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>rend()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td
								>Returns reverse iterator before smallest
								element</td
							>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>size()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns number of elements in the set</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>empty()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns true if set is empty</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>clear()</code></td>
							<td>O(n)</td>
							<td>Removes all elements from the set</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>

				<p><strong>Tips:</strong></p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						Use <code>lower_bound()</code> and <code
							>upper_bound()</code
						> to find ranges of values
					</li>
					<li>
						<code>*s.begin()</code> gives you the minimum element in O(1)
					</li>
					<li>
						<code>*s.rbegin()</code> gives you the maximum element in
						O(1)
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>

