			<div class="club-section">
				<h2>std::map</h2>
				<p>
					std::map is an associative container that contains a sorted
					set of unique key-value pairs. It allows us to store and
					retrieve elements based on their keys, very useful for
					mappings.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="club-section">
				<h2>std::map operations</h2>
				<p>
					In C++, <code>std::map</code> is a container that stores unique
					key-value pairs in sorted order by key. It's implemented as a
					balanced BST (typically Red-Black Tree), making it very useful
					for competitive programming.
				</p>
				<p>
					Internally it's a BST with pairs (key, value), so the
					operations are done over the keys and we can store other
					useful information in the value for fast queries.
				</p>

				<table class="operations-table">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Operation</th>
							<th>Complexity</th>
							<th>What it does</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>m[key] = value</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Inserts or updates key-value pair</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>m[key]</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Access value by key (creates if not exists)</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>at(key)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Access value by key (throws if not exists)</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>insert(&#123;key, value&#125;)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Inserts key-value pair (no duplicates)</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>erase(key)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Removes element by key</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>erase(it)</code></td>
							<td>O(1) amortized</td>
							<td>Removes element at iterator position</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>find(key)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td
								>Returns iterator to key, or end() if not found</td
							>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>count(key)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Returns 1 if key exists, 0 otherwise</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>lower_bound(key)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td
								>Returns iterator to first element with key ≥
								given key</td
							>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>upper_bound(key)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td
								>Returns iterator to first element with key >
								given key</td
							>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>begin()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns iterator to smallest key</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>end()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns iterator past the largest key</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>rbegin()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns reverse iterator to largest key</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>rend()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns reverse iterator before smallest key</td
							>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>size()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns number of key-value pairs</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>empty()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns true if map is empty</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>clear()</code></td>
							<td>O(n)</td>
							<td>Removes all elements from the map</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>

				<p><strong>Tips:</strong></p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						Use <code>m[key]</code> for quick access, but be careful -
						it creates the key if it doesn't exist
					</li>
					<li>
						Use <code>find()</code> or <code>count()</code> to check if
						a key exists without creating it
					</li>
					<li>
						<code>lower_bound()</code> and <code>upper_bound()</code
						> work on keys, not values
					</li>
					<li>
						Iterate with <code
							>for(auto &#91;key, value&#93; : m)</code
						> in C++17+
					</li>
				</ul>
