			<div class="club-section">
				<h2>std::multiset</h2>
				<p>
					std::multiset is similar to std::set, but it allows
					duplicate elements. This is extremely useful when you need
					to maintain a sorted collection with duplicates, like
					tracking frequencies while keeping elements sorted.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="club-section">
				<h2>std::multiset operations</h2>
				<p>
					In C++, <code>std::multiset</code> is a container that stores
					elements in sorted order and allows duplicates. It's implemented
					as a balanced BST (typically Red-Black Tree).
				</p>

				<table class="operations-table">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Operation</th>
							<th>Complexity</th>
							<th>What it does</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>insert(x)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Inserts element x (allows duplicates)</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>erase(x)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n + count)</td>
							<td>Removes ALL occurrences of element x</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>erase(it)</code></td>
							<td>O(1) amortized</td>
							<td>Removes single element at iterator position</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>find(x)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td
								>Returns iterator to one occurrence of x, or
								end() if not found</td
							>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>count(x)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n + count)</td>
							<td>Returns number of occurrences of x</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>lower_bound(x)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Returns iterator to first element ≥ x</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>upper_bound(x)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td>Returns iterator to first element > x</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>equal_range(x)</code></td>
							<td>O(log n)</td>
							<td
								>Returns pair of iterators &#91;lower_bound,
								upper_bound&#93;</td
							>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>begin()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns iterator to smallest element</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>end()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns iterator past the largest element</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>rbegin()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns reverse iterator to largest element</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>rend()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td
								>Returns reverse iterator before smallest
								element</td
							>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>size()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td
								>Returns total number of elements (including
								duplicates)</td
							>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>empty()</code></td>
							<td>O(1)</td>
							<td>Returns true if multiset is empty</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>clear()</code></td>
							<td>O(n)</td>
							<td>Removes all elements from the multiset</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>

				<p><strong>Tips:</strong></p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<code>erase(x)</code> removes ALL occurrences - use <code
							>erase(find(x))</code
						> to remove just one
					</li>
					<li>
						<code>count(x)</code> can be slow if there are many duplicates
						- consider using <code>equal_range()</code>
					</li>
					<li>
						Perfect for maintaining a sorted frequency list or
						priority queue with duplicates
					</li>
					<li>
						Use <code>equal_range(x)</code> to get all occurrences of
						x efficiently
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>

			<div class="club-section">
				<h2>A wild CSES problem appeared</h2>

				<a
